      question: 'Why do parameterized queries prevent SQL injection when escaping does not reliably?',
      answer: 'Prepared statements send the query structure to the database first, then bind parameter values separately, so input is ALWAYS treated as data and never parsed as SQL — injection is impossible by construction. Manual escaping must cover every encoding, quote style and context; one miss is exploitable. ORMs parameterize under the hood but raw fragments and string interpolation reintroduce the bug.',
      deepDive: 'Identifiers (table/column names) cannot be parameterized — handle a user-chosen sort column with an allow-list, not interpolation. Defense in depth: least-privilege DB accounts so a successful injection cannot DROP or read everything, and generic errors to blunt blind/error-based extraction.',
      followUps: ['How do you safely handle a user-chosen sort column?', 'What is blind/time-based SQLi?', 'Can ORMs be injectable?'],
