      id: 'q-cors-misconception',
      level: 'advanced',
      question: 'Is CORS a mechanism that protects your server? Explain.',
      answer: 'No — CORS relaxes the Same-Origin Policy in the BROWSER. SOP stops page JavaScript from reading cross-origin responses; CORS lets a server opt specific origins back in. The request still reaches and is processed by your server (which is why CSRF exists). CORS controls what malicious browser JS can READ, not what the server accepts — server-side authentication and authorization are the real protection (curl ignores CORS entirely).',
      deepDive: 'Credentials rule: Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true cannot combine with Allow-Origin: * — you must echo a specific origin, validated against an allow-list. Reflecting an arbitrary Origin header with credentials true effectively disables SOP for authenticated APIs. Preflight (OPTIONS) gates non-simple requests but is browser-enforced, not a server firewall.',
      followUps: ['Why is reflecting any Origin with credentials a vulnerability?', 'What triggers a CORS preflight?', 'Why does CORS not prevent CSRF?'],
