      question: 'How would you protect a login endpoint from brute force and credential stuffing?',
      answer: 'Layer controls: rate limit per account AND per IP (botnets defeat IP-only limits), add exponential backoff and CAPTCHA after a few failures, enforce MFA, and never reveal whether the username exists (uniform error and timing). Use a shared store (Redis) so limits hold across a horizontally-scaled fleet, return 429 with Retry-After, and monitor for failure spikes signaling credential stuffing.',
      deepDive: 'Algorithm choice: token bucket allows controlled bursts; sliding window is precise but heavier. Prefer adaptive friction and breached-password checks (Have I Been Pwned k-anonymity) over hard account lockouts, which enable lockout-DoS. A surge of failures across many accounts from distributed IPs is the credential-stuffing signature.',
      followUps: ['IP vs account-based limits — why both?', 'How do you avoid account-lockout DoS?', 'Token bucket vs sliding window?'],
